  "name": "24 HR URINARY GLUCOSE",
  "code": "1003",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p>A 24-hour urinary glucose test measures the total amount of glucose excreted in the urine over a full day. Normally, there is very little to no glucose in urine because the kidneys reabsorb glucose efficiently.</p>\n<p>Elevated glucose levels in urine (glucosuria) indicate:</p>\n<p>&nbsp;&bull; Increased blood glucose exceeding kidney reabsorption threshold, as seen in uncontrolled or poorly controlled diabetes mellitus.<br>&nbsp;&bull; Gestational diabetes during pregnancy.<br>&nbsp;&bull; Rare kidney disorders like renal glycosuria, where kidneys release glucose regardless of blood glucose levels.</p>\n<p>Clinical significance:</p>\n<p>&nbsp;&bull; Presence of glucose in urine is a marker of hyperglycemia or kidney tubular dysfunction.<br>&nbsp;&bull; Normal urine glucose does not rule out diabetes if blood glucose is high because glucose appears in urine only after blood glucose crosses a certain renal threshold (~160-180 mg/dL).<br>&nbsp;&bull; Slight, transient increases in urine glucose may occur after large meals or during stress.</p>",
